## 2026-08-28 — Open Room and Pit: architecture decisions (Tasks 1-8)

Two new piece types landed: `PieceType.Room` (paint a rectangle, get one open
space) and `PieceType.Pit` (a 3x3 shaft spanning up to 3 floors). The task-by-task
build log lives in `.superpowers/sdd/roompit-task-{1..7}-report.md`; commits
`2123fd0..b660698` on `feat/rooms-pits-traps`. This entry records the decisions
worth remembering, and why, rather than re-narrating each task.

**Room is stored per-tile, not as a rectangle — this is the load-bearing
choice.** Painting two opposite corners in the editor (`MazeEditor3D.PaintRoomRect`)
writes one independent `MazePiece` of type `Room` into *every* cell of the
rectangle; there is no rectangle object anywhere in the saved data.
`RoomGroups.Build` (new, `scripts/RoomGroups.cs`) flood-fills adjacent `Room`
tiles back into connected groups at build time, purely for geometry. This was
chosen over storing a rectangle/footprint object because `Room` declares
openings on all four faces at every rotation (`PieceDB.BaseOpenings`), so the
existing per-tile systems — `MazeValidator`'s BFS, `MazeSerializer` save/load,
gold cost accounting, and single-cell right-click deletion — all keep working
**with zero changes**. It also makes L-shaped rooms fall out for free (drag
twice, the flood-fill merges them into one group). A rectangle-object model
would have required every one of those systems to become footprint-aware.

**`PieceDB.Footprint(t)` / `PieceDB.FloorSpan(t)` are the only sanctioned way to
ask what volume a piece claims.** `Pit` is the first (and so far only)
`IsMultiCell` piece: one `MazePiece` sits at the shaft's centre (the anchor),
and `Footprint` returns the 9 `(dx,dy)` offsets of the 3x3 while `FloorSpan`
returns `{-1,0,+1}` floor offsets from the anchor, clipped at build/query time
to `DungeonBuilder.FloorMin/FloorMax` (`[-3,+3]`). Every consumer — editor
occupancy (`MazeEditor3D.TryGetPlacedPieceOnFloor`), validator connectivity
(`MazeValidator.IsGameReady`'s BFS), and geometry (`PitBuilder`) — resolves
volume through these two methods instead of hand-rolling its own 3x3/3-floor
math, so a future multi-cell piece type only has to implement `Footprint`/
`FloorSpan` and inherits editor occupancy + BFS connectivity for free. **Both
methods return a fresh copy of their backing array on every call** — an early
version returned the shared `static readonly` array directly, so one caller
mutating its result in place permanently corrupted that array for every other
piece type sharing it, for the rest of the process's life. Fixed before it
shipped (`3d0d667`), but worth remembering if a future `Footprint`-like helper
gets added elsewhere: always `.Clone()` a shared backing array before handing
it out.

**Room ceiling numbers:** `RoomBuilder.RoomSpringY = 11.0`, `RoomPeakY = 17.5`
(`scripts/RoomBuilder.cs`). `RoomPeakY` **must stay below
`DungeonBuilder.FloorHeight` (`CellHeight 14 + ArchRise 4 = 18.0`)** or a room's
vault punches into the floor slab above it. The corridor vault (spring 14, peak
18) is deliberately different — a room's drama comes from arch *span* across a
wide merged group, not height, since height is hard-capped by the floor above
in a way a single 10 m corridor cell never bumps into.

**`RoomGroups` is the seam between per-tile storage and per-space geometry.**
Nothing upstream of it (editor, validator, serializer) ever sees a "room" as
anything but N independent 1x1 `Room` tiles. `RoomBuilder.Build` consumes one
`RoomGroup` at a time and renders it as one merged space: one flat floor,
walls only on `RoomGroup.IsPerimeterFace` cells (interior tile-to-tile borders
inside a group stay open), doorways where `HasCorridorThrough` finds a
neighbouring piece's opening pointed at the room, a plinth along the wall
spans, and one continuous barrel vault. If a future feature needs to reason
about "a room" as a whole (not a tile), `RoomGroups.Build` is the place to look
— it is the only code that turns the flood-fill scatter of tiles back into a
shape.

**The `"Floor"` mesh-name prefix is load-bearing, and bit twice.**
`DungeonBuilder.AddMesh` (line ~500) picks the floor vs. wall material purely
by `name.StartsWith("Floor") || name.StartsWith("Stair")`. The first cut of
`RoomBuilder` named its floor mesh `RoomFloor_...`, which starts with `"Room"`
— so every room floor silently rendered with the stone/wall texture instead of
the floor texture (`810e99f`). Room and Pit meshes are now named `FloorRoom_`/
`FloorPit_` (prefix first), not `RoomFloor_`/`PitFloor_`. Any future piece type
that emits its own floor mesh needs to name it with the `"Floor"` prefix
literally first, not as a suffix of the piece name.

**Geometry is emitted per (cell, floor), never merged per room or per shaft.**
The plan's first draft of both `RoomBuilder` and `PitBuilder` built one shared
`SurfaceTool`/mesh for the whole room group or whole shaft on one
`StaticBody3D`. `RoomBuilder` actually shipped that way and had to be undone in
a follow-up commit (`810e99f`); `PitBuilder` never did — the plan was patched
before that task ran, so it was written per-cell from its first commit
(`827ef74`). The correct shape is one body + mesh set per cell
(`Cell_{cx}_{cy}_{floor}`, matching `DungeonBuilder.BuildFloor`'s own
convention), required by the existing doc comment at `DungeonBuilder.cs:156-160`
(now shifted slightly, still present near `Build()`'s per-floor loop): this
renderer's GL-compatibility backend only lights a mesh from its bounding box's
*centre*, so a large merged mesh has its corners under-lit or dark. A 3x2+ room
or a 3x3x3 pit shaft is exactly the shape that triggers it. The vault/dome
still reads as one seamless arc across cell boundaries despite the per-cell
split, because `ArchY` is always called with the *group's* (or shaft's) world
bounds, never a per-cell `x0/x1` — two adjacent cells evaluate the same pure
function at their shared edge and agree on height.

**`PieceVariants` decorates by matching `Cell_{x}_{y}_{floor}` body names, and
that collided with multi-cell pieces.** It looks up a body by the piece's own
stored `(X, Y, Floor)` — for `Pit` that's the shaft's anchor cell, which
usually has no floor slab at all (the anchor floor is the shaft's open middle
level). Result: a rug and occasional clutter prop rendered floating in mid-air
in the open shaft. Fix (`1c503df`, `scripts/PieceVariants.cs`): `ApplyVariant`
now returns immediately for any `PieceDB.IsMultiCell` piece — verified there is
no floor-anchored decor that's safe to keep for Pit, since its anchor cell has
no floor in the common case. `Room` was *not* blanket-skipped: its per-cell
bodies genuinely do sit on real floor geometry (`RoomBuilder` builds one body
per cell at the group's floor height, same convention as a corridor cell), so
rugs/dropped clutter are fine and were left on. Only the four wall-anchored
decor calls (`AddCofferBeams`, `AddSconces`, `AddCornerProps`,
`AddMidCorridorProp`) are explicitly guarded off for `Room` — they were
*already* accidental no-ops for it (both `Room` and `Pit` declare all-four-
sides-open in `PieceDB.GetOpenings`, so every "which walls are closed" check in
those functions already evaluated false), but that protection was incidental
to `GetOpenings` being a connectivity table, not a reflection of
`RoomBuilder`'s real per-cell wall placement. The explicit guard converts an
accident into a real invariant, and leaves a comment for whoever adds the next
multi-cell piece type explaining the coupling. **Known follow-up, not fixed:**
a room cell that's a stair's landing cell (`RoomBuilder.IsStairLandingCell`)
also has no floor slab, but `PieceVariants.Apply`'s signature doesn't currently
receive the stair lookup needed to detect that one cell — a much narrower gap
than the Pit bug, flagged for later.

**Validator BFS must collect *every* neighbour per direction, not the first.**
`MazeValidator.IsGameReady`'s BFS neighbour search used to do
`nb = cand; break;` on the first hit in a direction. Harmless for single-cell
pieces (only one possible neighbour per direction), wrong for `Pit`: a 3x3
shaft exposes three perimeter cells per side per floor, across up to three
floors, so more than one corridor can legitimately land on the same pit face —
either at a different footprint offset on the same floor, or the same offset
on a different floor. The old code silently dropped every candidate after the
first found in enumeration order, so a maze that was actually fully connected
got misreported as "No path from Start to Exit" and was silently excluded from
the online lobby's maze selector. Fixed (`b5f659d`) by collecting every
distinct unvisited candidate per direction into a dictionary keyed by anchor
`(x,y,floor)` before applying the stair cross-floor-entry guard to each. The
pit's own volume is still explicitly excluded from its own neighbour search
(`cand == piece`) — that guard is what keeps a pit from becoming a
connectivity wildcard, and the "a disconnected pit does NOT make an
unreachable Exit valid" regression test exists specifically to catch a future
rewrite that weakens it. Do not remove or loosen that check.

**The editor refuses rather than destroys.** `MazeEditor3D.RemovePiece`
resolves a clicked cell to its owning piece and deletes that piece's *entire*
record — correct for right-click delete, but two other call sites used to lean
on it as an implicit "clear whatever's here first": painting a room rectangle
over any corner of an existing pit's footprint silently deleted the whole
3-floor pit before painting room tiles over the gap, and dropping/placing an
ordinary piece onto a pit's footprint sailed through validation (`Pit` is open
on every face, so it always looked "connected") and then silently deleted the
pit underneath it. Fixed (`b660698`) the same way `Start`/`Exit` are already
protected: `RoomPaintSkipsCell` now blocks any cell resolving to an
`IsMultiCell` piece and reports how many cells were skipped and why;
`IsValidPlacement` rejects placing *anything* onto an occupied multi-cell
footprint before it ever reaches `RemovePiece`. This editor has no undo, so
silent destructive side effects from an unrelated action are the worst class
of bug it can have — any future occupancy-adjacent editor change should check
whether it can reach `RemovePiece` through a multi-cell piece's footprint
without the user asking to delete that piece.

