## 2026-08-28 — Third-person readability: opponents you can actually read

Driven by a playtest requirement: in a 1v1 you must be able to tell, at a glance,
where the other player is aiming and what they are doing. Built
`test/TestThirdPerson.tscn` — films a real PlayerController from an opponent's
vantage through idle → aim up → aim down → shoot → reload → run → run+shoot — and
then fixed what it showed. Four bugs, three of them invisible until filmed.

**1. Remote opponents rendered EMPTY-HANDED.** `PickupWeapon` only made the
hand-attached weapon visible when `_tpMode` was on, so a remote puppet — which is
never in `_tpMode` — showed no weapon at all. You could not tell what you were up
against. The gating was a leftover from before the visual-layer split; the body is
already on layer 2, which the owner's own FP camera excludes, so the hand weapon
can simply always be visible while armed.

**2. No gun stance at all.** `DetermineState` never returned a gun state, so the
character stood in a neutral arms-at-sides idle with a gun stuck in its hand, and
`PlayShoot`/`PlayReload` were *full-body* one-shots — shooting mid-sprint snapped
the legs to standing.

Fixed with an **upper-body animation layer**: the AnimationTree root is now a
filtered `AnimationNodeBlend2` whose input 0 is the existing locomotion state
machine and input 1 is a small gun state machine (aim blendspace / shoot /
reload). 48 of 59 bone tracks route to the gun layer. Legs run while arms aim,
shoot and reload independently — which is exactly what makes a charging opponent
distinguishable from a reloading one.

- **Bone names are Godot humanoid-profile names**, not the Unreal names in the
  source rig: `Hips`, `Chest`, `UpperChest`, `RightUpperArm`, `RightHand`… There is
  no `Spine`. This is also why the hand bone resolves as `RightHand`. The filter is
  written as an EXCLUSION list (`Root`, `Hips`, and the eight leg bones) because
  the upper body is ~50 bones once fingers are counted and an inclusion list would
  silently drop whatever was forgotten.
- **A programmatically-built nested state machine has no entry point.** Without an
  explicit `Start → GunAim` transition the gun layer sat on nothing and the
  filtered bones fell back to rest pose — a T-pose from the chest up.

**3. The weapon was held wrong** because the bone-attachment holder still carried
`(0, 180, -90)`, a correction for the old blade-along-Z sword mesh. Removed it;
