- `BuildFpGun` already applies the +90° Y that points the barrel down -Z, so the
  hand holder must stay at IDENTITY rotation — rotating there too double-applies it
  and turns the gun sideways. Same arrangement as the third-person holder.

**Final alignment is [Export]-tunable.** `FpGunInHandPos/Rot` and
`FpArmsPosHip/Ads` are exported and re-applied every frame, so they can be dragged
in the remote inspector DURING PLAY. Blind capture-and-look iteration was costing
~40 s per guess for what is pure taste; this makes it seconds.

## 2026-08-28 — AWP scope actually works; sprint carry; HUD lookup bug

**The scope never worked because the HUD widgets could not find the gun.**
`ScopeOverlay` and `SpreadCrosshair` walked TWO ancestor levels to locate
`GunCombat`, but the real depth is Control → HUDRoot → PlayerHUD →
PlayerController — three. `StaminaRing` counted three and worked; the other two
resolved to null forever.

Because both widgets degrade gracefully, this failed **completely silently**:
- `ScopeOverlay` saw `IsAiming == false` always, so it never drew a single pixel.
- `SpreadCrosshair` fell back to `_gun?.SpreadDegrees ?? 1f` — it has been drawing
  at a **fixed 1° spread since it was written**. The "crosshair tracks live spread"
  claim was never true in play.

Replaced the hand-counted chains with `HudLookup.FindGun`, which walks ancestors
until one owns a `GunCombat`. Depth-independent, so re-parenting the HUD cannot
break it again.

**Verified, not eyeballed.** `test/TestScope.tscn` films from the player's OWN FP
camera (creates no camera of its own) down a pillar range. Printed FOV proves the
zoom: `100.0 → 25.0 in a single frame` (~0.05 s) on scope-in. My read of the
screenshots had said "no zoom" — wrong, the lens crop hid it. Numbers beat pixels.

Also: the arms were visible INSIDE the lens. `AnimateFpGun` now hides
`_fpArmsModel` as well as the gun once `_scopeBlend` completes.

**Halo-style sprint carry** added: weapon dropped and canted while sprinting,
driven by `_sprintBlend` and explicitly overridden by ADS (you cannot sprint and
scope at once). First attempt used a 30° rotation on the arms root and threw the
hands out of frame entirely — **the arms root is a whole body's origin ~1.6 m below
the camera, so rotating it swings the hands through a huge arc.** Sprint carry is
now position-dominant with only a slight cant.

**Still open:** guns are not seated convincingly in the hands. One shared
`FpGunInHandPos/Rot` cannot suit seven different weapons, and the UAL1 hand poses
have generic finger positions that do not wrap a grip. Proper fix is per-gun
in-hand offsets plus per-gun hand poses authored in Blender. The global offsets are
`[Export]`ed and applied per frame so they can at least be dialled live.

## 2026-08-28 — Movement-hindrance traps: eight traps, all cost time, not health

Eight new `PieceType`s landed: `TrapIce` (Slick Floor), `TrapTar` (Slow Field),
`TrapDark` (Darkness Zone), `TrapDoor` (Trapdoor), `TrapGate` (One-Way Gate),
`TrapPush` (Push Tile), `TrapPortal` (Teleport Pad), `TrapGrease` (Greased
Walls). Task-by-task build log: `.superpowers/sdd/traps-task-{1..6}-report.md`,
commits `5d03e0b..23f9d9e` on `feat/rooms-pits-traps`. This entry records the
decisions worth remembering, and why, rather than re-narrating each task.

**Design rule, stated once in `scripts/TrapDB.cs` and held everywhere below
it: no trap deals damage.** The game is a timed escape, so the currency a trap
spends is the opponent's clock, not their HP — a trapdoor drops you a floor
unhurt, a gate re-routes you, a teleport sends you backwards, ice and tar just
make you slower to react. Every trap file's own header comment repeats this so
it survives being read out of context.

**Every trap declares `Dir.N | Dir.S` openings (`PieceDB.BaseOpenings`) — the
architectural keystone that kept this whole feature out of the fragile code.**
That single choice means `PieceDB.GetOpenings`, `MazeValidator`'s BFS,
`DungeonBuilder.AddPiece`, and the editor's placement rules all see a trap as
an ordinary `Straight` corridor. None of that connection/rotation logic
changed. A trap is corridor geometry, built for free, plus dressing
(`TrapBuilder.Decorate`) plus, for four of the eight, a behaviour node
(`TrapBehaviours.Decorate`) — never a new piece shape.

**`TrapZone` (`scripts/TrapZone.cs`) is a bag of multipliers that combines
multiplicatively across every zone the player is currently inside.**
`PlayerController.RecomputeTrapModifiers` folds `_trapZones` down to
`_trapFrictionMul`/`_trapSpeedMul`/`_trapAccelMul`/`_trapPush`/`_trapNoWallRun`
by multiplying (friction/speed/accel) or OR-ing (push, wall-run block), so
standing in ice inside a darkness zone applies both without either zone
knowing the other exists. With zero zones active every multiplier is exactly
`1f` and push is `Vector3.Zero`, so the five call sites this wired into in
`PlayerController.cs` (top speed, ground accel, ground friction, a push
`Velocity +=` before `MoveAndSlide()`, and the wall-run gate) are each a
multiplicative or boolean identity — movement is bit-for-bit unchanged when no
trap is present. `RecomputeTrapModifiers` also purges any zone whose node was
freed while the player was still "inside" it (`IsInstanceValid`), so a trap
cell being torn down mid-rebuild can't leave a dangling reference.

**A trap's trigger volume is `CellHeight * 0.9f` tall (≈12.6 m,
`TrapBuilder.AddZone`/`TrapBehaviours` trigger shapes) — deliberately almost
the full cell height, not a floor-flush slab.** The game's core movement tech
is bhop, and a thin slab would let an airborne player skip a trap entirely by
jumping across the cell without the `Area3D` ever registering
`BodyEntered`. Tall triggers mean jumping over ice or tar doesn't dodge it —
the whole point of a "movement-hindrance" trap is that it costs time even
against the exact tech that would otherwise trivialize it.

**`FogVolume` does nothing under this project's renderer.** This project sets
`renderer/rendering_method="gl_compatibility"` in `project.godot`, and Godot's
volumetric fog is Forward+ only. Building the Darkness Zone as a `FogVolume`
would have compiled, run, and rendered literally nothing. The working
substitute (`TrapBehaviours.AddDarkness`) is six unshaded, alpha-blended
"curtain" quads spanning the corridor cross-section, spaced through the
cell's depth. The first substitute — one hollow alpha box shell — was also
wrong, caught only by looking at captured frames: a shell only tints a camera
ray that *crosses* one of its six faces, so a player standing **inside** the
cell (exactly where they are once they walk into the trap) saw the far end of
the corridor completely unobscured. Stacking several curtains means any
forward- or backward-looking ray from anywhere in the cell crosses multiple of
them before it can reach a lit neighbour, building up occlusion with distance
the way real fog does.

**The trap floor overlay sits at `yBase + 0.08` (`TrapBuilder.AddFloorOverlay`),
not the more obvious `0.04`.** `PieceVariants.AddRugSlab` puts a decorative
rug on every non-stair cell — including trap cells — centred at `yBase +
0.03` with height `0.05`, so its top face sits at `yBase + 0.055`. At `0.04`
the opaque rug renders in front of the overlay and hides most of it; only a
sliver at each edge (where the narrower rug doesn't reach) showed through,
which is how the bug first looked. `0.08` clears the rug's top face with
margin.

**A trapdoor refuses to build (or be placed) on the world's bottom floor,
because there is no automatic out-of-world recovery in this game.** The only
recovery mechanism anywhere in `PlayerController.cs` is manual — holding `R`
for `ResetHoldThreshold` teleports back to `DungeonBuilder.MazeStartPosition`
— and that code path returns early for `IsRemotePuppet`, so a remote
multiplayer puppet has no reset at all. `TrapdoorNode` only hides/disables the
cell's own floor and lets gravity do the rest; it never checks that a floor
exists below. Fixed both ways: `MazeEditor3D.PlacePiece` refuses a new
`TrapDoor` at `_floor <= FlMin` (same pattern as the pre-existing
`StairsDown`-at-bottom guard), and `TrapBehaviours.AddTrapdoor` no-ops as a
runtime backstop when `p.Floor <= DungeonBuilder.FloorMin` — covering a maze
saved before the editor rule existed.

**`MazePiece.LinkId` (`scripts/MazeData.cs`) is the first per-piece state
field.** It defaults to `0` ("unpaired", inert everywhere it's checked), so
every old saved maze keeps loading with every piece unpaired. `MazeSerializer`
has no `JsonStringEnumConverter` registered anywhere — `System.Text.Json`
serializes `PieceType` numerically, not by name — worth knowing before
hand-writing a save-file fixture; a literal like `"Type":"Straight"` throws on
load, it has to be `"Type":2`. Picking up or dropping a teleport pad in the
editor almost lost this field on the first pass — `PickUpPiece`/`DropPiece`/
`CancelHolding` rebuild a fresh `MazePiece` from a handful of stashed fields
that didn't originally include `LinkId` — fixed by threading `_heldLinkId`
through all three.

**`TeleportPadNode` partner matching is scoped to the owning `DungeonBuilder`,
not just `LinkId`.** `OnlineDungeonArena` runs two independently-authored
mazes live at once, and `MazeEditor3D._nextLinkId` restarts at 1 for every
fresh maze, so two mazes that each contain one teleport pair both use
`LinkId = 1` — the common case, not an edge case. Matching on `LinkId` alone
would silently teleport a player into the opponent's dungeon. Fixed by giving
`TeleportPadNode` an `OwnerBuilder` reference (set from the `owner` the pad
was built with) and requiring both `LinkId` and `OwnerBuilder` to match before
resolving a partner.

**`MazeEditor3D.Palette` is a hand-maintained array, separate from `PieceDB`,
and nothing enforced that a new `PieceType` ever reached it.** All eight
traps (and, before them, `Room`/`Pit`) were fully wired into `PieceDB` —
openings, colors, labels, gold cost, category — without a single line added
to `Palette`, so the TRAPS tab in a running editor was empty until this was
caught during self-review, well after the trap logic itself was tested and
working. Fixed by extending `Palette` from 6 entries to 16 and adding a
"Palette coverage" block to `test/TestPieceCategories.cs` that requires every
`PieceType` to be either in `Palette` or in an explicit exclusion list
(`Start`, `Exit`, the legacy `Stairs`) — so the next forgotten piece type
fails a headless test instead of rendering a blank tab. The same fix pass
also gave `BuildPieceInto`'s color switch a `TrapDB.IsTrap`/`Room`/`Pit` arm
(trap tiles had been rendering with the same tint as plain Structure pieces
until then) and made the glyph mark's contrast direction adaptive
(`lum > 0.6f` darkens instead of lightens) — the pale-cyan Ice and amber Door
tiles were washing out a glyph that always lightened toward white.

## 2026-08-28 — Scoped-in-air accuracy, AWP one-shot head, measured damage profile

**Scoping now steadies you mid-air.** `CurrentSpread` skips the airborne penalty
entirely while aiming, so a jumping scoped shot is a real play rather than a coin
flip. Measured: airborne AWP spread goes from **6.05° hipfire to 0.00° scoped**.
Nothing ever blocked scoping in the air — the penalty just made it pointless.

**AWP headshot is lethal as a RULE, not as arithmetic.** `GunDef.OneShotHeadshot`
makes the shot deal the full health pool outright. Tuning damage past 100/1.5 would
have worked today and silently stopped working the moment `MaxHealth` or `Damage`
changed. Asserted every run of `test/TestDamageProfile.tscn`.

**Measured the real damage-vs-distance profile** (new harness: 12 shots per weapon
per distance at centre mass, averaged INCLUDING misses):

```
             3m    6m   10m   15m   20m   25m   30m   40m
MAC-10     18.0  18.0  18.0  18.0  13.5  15.0  12.8   5.2
Sidearm    22.0  22.0  22.0  22.0  22.0  22.0  22.0  22.0
Shotgun    64.0  57.3  37.7  16.0   8.7   5.0   4.7   0.0
AWP        55.0 ───────────────── flat ─────────────── 55.0
AK-47      32.0 ──────────── flat ──────────── 32.0   26.7
```

**There is NO damage falloff anywhere in the codebase.** Damage is
distance-independent; `Range` is a hard cutoff, not a curve. Every decline above is
caused purely by SPREAD widening until rounds miss the silhouette. Worth stating
plainly because the shotgun's curve looks exactly like a falloff table and is not one.

**Testing gotcha:** a body added to the tree this frame is not in the physics space
yet, so a raycast at it silently misses. The first AWP-headshot assertion failed for
that reason alone. Rule checks now run from `_PhysicsProcess` after a few frames.

Published the reference as an artifact (charts + tables, measured numbers only).

## 2026-08-28 — Free-movement accuracy pass + grip auto-seating

Target feel: **Rivals** (the Roblox FPS). Its model is a *slight* movement penalty
with learnable recoil patterns doing the real work — not Palisade's heavy random
cone. Did steps 2 and 3 of that translation (tighter cones, weaker speed coupling);
deterministic recoil patterns deliberately left for later.

**Added `GunDef.SpreadFreeSpeed` (5.5 m/s = walk).** Spread previously grew
linearly FROM ZERO, so merely walking cost the AK 5× its accuracy and the AWP
**122×** — repositioning threw the fight. Only speed above walk pace is taxed now.

Cones tightened across the board. Measured spread° by speed:

```
                 still   walk5.5  sprint8   bhop16   air-ADS
MAC-10            0.70     0.70     1.15     2.59     —
Sidearm           0.25     0.25     0.60     1.72     —
Shotgun           3.40     3.40     3.70     4.66     —
AWP               0.03     0.03     1.65     6.86    0.00
AK-47             0.35     0.35     0.85     2.45     —
```

A torso at 10 m is ±2.3°, so everything but the shotgun is now a reliable hit at a
sprint (was 5.4° for the MAC-10, ~18% hit rate). Bhop still costs you — rifles land
right at the threshold — and the AWP keeps its identity as the stillness weapon
(0.03° → 6.86°, a 229× swing). Side effect: MAC-10 now holds full damage to 30 m
instead of decaying from 20 m.

**Grip auto-seating — measured, not tuned.** Two failed approaches first:
1. Aligning the gun's trigger to the holder origin put the grip at the **wrist**,
   because the fingers close well forward of the hand bone.
2. Measuring the grip point in Blender would not transfer — Godot's humanoid
   retargeting can change bone rest orientations.

What works: compute it **live in Godot** from the posed skeleton. Average the ten
right-hand finger joint positions (`RightIndexProximal` … `RightThumbMetacarpal`)
to get where the fist actually closes, then shift the model so its `trigger` node
lands there. Per-gun automatic, survives animation changes, no hand-tuned offsets.

Key insight behind it: the UAL1 hand is already curled around an **invisible
pistol** — that is what the clip was animated holding. The job was never to invent
a grip pose, only to find the one already there and put each gun's grip on it.

Must run after the skeleton has a real pose (`AnimateFpGun`), not at build time —
measuring in `RebuildFpGun` reads the rest pose.

## 2026-08-28 — Room/Pit correction: stairs land ON a room's floor, not through it

Final whole-branch review caught two Critical, merge-blocking bugs in the
Room/Pit feature (2026-08-27). Both were bugs in the plan that produced the
work, not implementer slips — the implementer built exactly what was asked.

**Pits were sealed off from every maze — unreachable, yet certified
game-ready.** `DungeonBuilder.Build` built its piece lookup ANCHOR-ONLY
(`lookup[(p.X, p.Y, p.Floor)] = p`), but a Pit's anchor is the CENTRE of its
3x3 footprint, so all four of the anchor's neighbours are the pit's own cells
— nothing outside a pit can ever resolve it through the anchor key. Every
real connection to a pit runs through a PERIMETER cell. `PitBuilder.HasEntry`
(footprint-aware) correctly punched a doorway on the pit's side of that
boundary; the neighbouring corridor or room, resolving the pit cell against
the anchor-only lookup, found nothing and built a full-height cap wall
exactly coplanar with that doorway. `MazeValidator` used a SEPARATE,
correctly footprint-aware lookup for its own BFS, so it certified these
mazes as valid while the built geometry sealed every pit shut. Three
subsystems (`DungeonBuilder`, `MazeValidator`, `MazeEditor3D.
TryGetPlacedPieceOnFloor`), two conventions.

Fix: extracted the ONE shared implementation of "which piece occupies
(x, y, floor)" into `scripts/MazeLookup.cs` (`MazeLookup.Build`), routed
through `PieceDB.Footprint`/`FloorSpan` rather than special-casing `Pit` by
name. Both `DungeonBuilder.Build` and `MazeValidator.IsGameReady` now call
it. `MazeValidator`'s visited-set canonicalisation (a pit is one BFS node,
not 27) is unchanged — canonicalisation is the validator's own concern, the
shared helper only builds the raw cell map.

**A room deletes the floor slab the player is supposed to land on.**
`RoomBuilder.IsStairLandingCell` omitted a room cell's floor slab whenever a
stair on an adjacent floor's cross face landed there, on the theory that the
room slab would otherwise cap the stairwell shut. That premise doesn't match
the actual stair geometry: `DungeonBuilder.AddStairGeometry` builds every
tread INSIDE THE STAIR'S OWN CELL, and the top tread's `yHi` already reaches
exactly `yBase + FloorHeight` — the landing floor's own level — at the stair
cell's boundary. The room's floor slab on the adjacent landing cell, at that
same height, IS the landing surface, not a lid over the stairwell. Removing
it meant the player walked off the top tread into open air with no
out-of-world recovery.

Fix: deleted `IsStairLandingCell` and its call site. `RoomBuilder.Build` now
always emits a cell's floor slab, exactly like a corridor cell always does.
"Stairs come into a room from below automatically" continues to work with no
special case — it never actually needed one.

**A room's perimeter wall stopped 6.5 m below its own vault, on every
room.** `RoomBuilder.AddPerimeterWall` capped a solid (non-doorway) wall face
at `RoomSpringY` (11 m) and, on a doorway face, only closed the gap up to
`FloorHeight` across the 6 m opening — never the flanking stubs, never the
solid case. But `AddCellVault`'s arch is a function of the whole ROOM
GROUP's bounds, so on the faces perpendicular to the vault's run the ceiling
reaches `RoomPeakY` (17.5 m) directly above where the wall stopped, leaving
an unsealed band up to 6.5 m tall across the full 10 m of that face — on
EVERY room, including a 1x1. Fixed by emitting an unconditional full-width
panel from `RoomDoorH` to `FloorHeight` on every perimeter face regardless of
doorway/solid, in addition to (not instead of) the existing
doorway/solid wall logic for the 0..`RoomSpringY` band.

**Painting a room could silently delete a stair with no warning and no
undo.** `MazeEditor3D.RoomPaintSkipsCell` resolved occupancy same-floor-only
(`TryGetPlacedPieceOnFloor(x, y, _floor, …)`), but `PaintRoomRect`'s
`RemovePiece` resolves cross-floor-aware (`TryGetActiveFloorPiece`) — so a
room rectangle over a cell where a stair's own vertical shaft is visually
present on the floor above/below (same X/Y, adjacent floor) deleted that
stair without the skip-and-report path ever seeing it. Fixed by making
`RoomPaintSkipsCell` use the same cross-floor-aware resolution `RemovePiece`
uses, and adding stairs to the skip-and-report set alongside `Pit` (separate
`blockedByStair` out-param; `PaintRoomRect`'s status line now names both
reasons when both occur).

**Added `test/TestRoomPitConnectivity.cs`** — a geometry-connectivity suite
distinct from the existing (entirely unit-level) `TestRoomPitLogic.cs`. It
builds real `DungeonBuilder` output for small hand-built mazes and inspects
the actual emitted `Mesh` triangles (via `Mesh.GetFaces()`, walking the
`Cell_{x}_{y}_{floor}` / `Wall_*`/`FloorRoom_*`/`WallRoom_*` node-naming
convention the builders already use — no new internal hook needed) rather
than re-testing the logic tables. Confirmed each of the three new assertions
fails against the pre-fix code and passes after: the pit-adjacency check
failed against the anchor-only lookup, the floor-slab check failed against
`IsStairLandingCell`'s omission, and the vault-seal check failed against the
old `RoomSpringY`-capped wall (top Y stuck at 11.00 instead of reaching
18.00).
